>> old programs by now???  Usually, there are options that make a compiler
>> backward compatible, but only up to a certain point.
>
> Yes, but for C that point is far, far away. Nethack is still written
> in pre-ANSI C if I recall correctly. That's something gcc and other C
> compilers on Unix happily accept -- because there is a lot of old code
> out there which people expect to compile.
>
> And now I guess I *have* to give 2.3e a try. I think it was the first
> version I played on my Amiga, too ...

I did, and I was wrong. There *are* some things in the 2.3e source code
that apparently *have* to be fixed, like using foo() and later in the
file declaring it static.  Others can simply be disabled, like the
'inline' keyword (Nethack uses it as a variable name).

I won't try to finish the compile, but here's a patch which makes
roughly 3/4 of the files build on Linux.  Most of the remaining errors
are boring 'static' problems, but there are some others I haven't
looked into. And of course I haven't tried to link or run the damned
thing.

/Jorgen


